m of Calculus" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048498 #edb/ref 1033 613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048498 #edb/ref 1034 0.9277065292880609 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048498 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a4b9d7e7-30a6-550b-9a00-c1803847c09c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1031 "Integrating Rates of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1033 2512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049952 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "20d8e368-2cd0-5f3b-ab4b-9db7029241aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428996 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428997 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428998 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428999 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429000 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1031 "The Integral as an Accumulation Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1033 333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1034 1.2813661251860098 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050200 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "35dc7585-dde7-500e-a85d-f3d54f38e249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429566 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1033 6691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050707 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "605a21e1-c415-5cc1-9181-c8f48db5f4ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147333 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147333 #edb/ref 1071 "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\n- Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\n- Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147333 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147333 #edb/ref 1074 "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147334 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147334 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147334 #edb/ref 1074 "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147335 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147335 #edb/ref 1071 "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147335 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147397 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147398 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147399 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1045 "Integrating Rates of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8898632a-f568-581b-9193-aeb177e79e44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1061 13518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62e34955-26a4-56d7-b473-2f2540475511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1061 13519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98d275b2-d6f2-53c5-b4d7-cae25b562f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1061 13520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2755fa08-44ef-5ef5-b8c8-6470c68e446a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1061 13521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "abe470e8-0b76-56c6-9c15-a1d10e391c4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148239 #edb/ref 1061 13522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d3cad10-c38a-51fd-b011-83786859f767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a tank is filling up with water, and the rate at which water flows into the tank is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 600e^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the water flow started. \n\nSuppose we want to know how much water enters the tank between $1$ and $2$ hours. \n\nLet's take note of the following:\n\n- We're interested in the amount of water that enters the tank between $t=1$ and $t=2.$ In other words, we want the **net change in volume** between these times. We're *not* finding the total volume of water in the tank.\n\n- We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$\n\nTo find the net change in volume, we integrate $r(t)$ between the two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}600{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{600}{3}{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={-200{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-200({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-3}) \\\\  & =200({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-6}) \\\\  & =200{e}^{-6}({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1065 2744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60a3bf2a-7142-575f-8381-dfb70827c7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1064 "If we're interested to find the **total number** or **total amount** of some quantity, we should use the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nLet's go back to the earlier example with the shopping mall (Example 1). Suppose that there are $2\\,000$ people inside the mall at the moment that it opened. How many people in total are inside the mall after $2$ hours?\n\nWe already know that the **rate of change** in the number of people is $R(t).$ Therefore, the **total number of people** in the mall at time $t$, which we'll call $T(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: You'll notice that we changed the variable of integration from $t$ to $x.$ This is to ensure that $T$ comes out as a function of $t,$ and also so we don't get confused. When we have a definite integral, it does not matter which variable we use!\n\nTo work out how many people are inside the mall at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\,000+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{850} \\\\  & =2\\,000+850 \\\\  & =2\\,850\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1065 2745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02950c6c-bf43-5c34-885c-8da2caa06fbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Total Amounts Given Rates of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317884 #edb/ref 1080 "21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317884 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317884 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5dedb264-eb4b-554b-b1fc-c33940aacd59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318250 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318250 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318250 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf14b68b-61e2-566a-924c-53e4c88d9ee2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318305 #edb/ref 1080 "260-3t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dcac5d0e-f796-5fa5-9733-a3ca8c3b5eff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318423 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318423 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318423 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318423 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "014eb3f4-5b0d-5bdd-b80d-f837749852b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318474 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318474 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318474 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186318305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318474 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "17091112-eedf-50be-b897-838934035785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318648 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318648 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318648 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318648 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186318250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318648 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6858d1c2-03bc-54aa-aad0-5ca01fbf3806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319358 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319358 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319358 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319358 #edb/ref 1071 "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319358 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "894497b2-7451-5370-bf78-0958e2308d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319358 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1071 "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "799a9b42-4f6e-5c6d-8236-7829c2135c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319361 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355288 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $230$ fish after $5$ weeks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355288 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355288 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "469565fb-6d27-567b-b115-17fe2967823a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355726 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $150$ people after $5$ hours" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355726 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355726 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "797df782-68e4-5946-a5d3-719aacd38fd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355896 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $200$ people after $2$ hours" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b39583c-7632-5314-aab0-b5c8f4813e75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355945 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $230$ fish after $1$ week" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355945 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355945 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90fa05bc-36a8-547f-aaf2-47eabb226e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355970 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $200$ people after $4$ hours" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355970 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355970 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "944fd6a9-bc5d-51fc-ae21-9b1361fccffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356033 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9a1e95e1-91bb-5808-97fa-1c6f520895f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356053 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $500$ people after $1$ hour" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356053 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356053 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c5a5011-dcd0-5823-8c28-0c3a166590aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356086 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a058f107-94e7-532a-8e45-31294473dddc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356509 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $215$ fish after $4$ weeks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356509 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356509 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d342dcb4-5a95-5454-8d7c-b07c177b7c00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356667 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $218$ fish after $2$ weeks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e59883a6-26da-5b2d-a2ad-67a91b6e4325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357118 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "715ea109-f7b4-5a47-ad9e-f36f04af9b6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357278 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c819c218-6e65-5a0e-be39-91f469445031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357474 #edb/ref 1071 "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f28ae95-03b9-593b-a6e6-a24580ae5aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357474 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357475 #edb/ref 1071 "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0121e26f-4108-56d0-ac26-f4c71b23dab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357475 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
